  "published": "2026-04-25T01:13:10.383Z",
  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "v1.1.6 stable: Two tracker-signaling bug fixes since 1.1.5. (1) `BinaryJsonSerializer` (client outbound) + `TrackerSignalingServer._readOpts` (server inbound) now explicitly set `TypeInfoResolver = new DefaultJsonTypeInfoResolver()` so announce serialization works under AOT/trimmed/file-based `dotnet run script.cs` hosts (new in .NET 10). Before this, the first announce threw `InvalidOperationException: Reflection-based serialization has been disabled for this application.` and was swallowed by the `_ = _discovery.AnnounceAsync(...)` fire-and-forget - C# clients silently never registered with trackers under reflection-disabled hosts. (2) `TrackerSignalingServer.HandleWebSocketAsync` now skips the `AllowedOrigins` check when the request's Origin header is empty. The allowlist is documented as browser-origin abuse protection (\"Origin is set by the browser and can be spoofed by non-browser clients\"); a missing Origin means a non-browser client (desktop C# `ClientWebSocket`, Node.js `ws` without explicit Origin, curl, etc.) that cannot be abused from a hostile page. Without this, any deployment with a populated allowlist 403'd every legitimate desktop / CLI / backend consumer. Caught by the new SpawnDev.WebTorrent `interop_test/js_webtorrent_liveswarm.cs` harness + the full PlaywrightMultiTest sweep. New `OriginAllowlist_E2E_MissingOriginBypassesList` E2E test locks the new behavior. Browser allowlist enforcement unchanged. PlaywrightMultiTest full sweep **323/0/3 in 2m 12s**.",
